r encoding, 6d-b's recover-from-last-durable-checkpoint semantics, module-pins admission on the resume side); (b) import = fetch by address, verify, admit (module pins), lower, resume; (c) command surface likely wants both save-and-continue and save-and-stop variants. FOUR DESIGN QUESTIONS the Tuesday loop must settle: (1) custody/split-brain — a published CESK state is durable and addressable; who may resume it, and what prevents two hosts resuming the same pack concurrently (occurrence/successor/arbitration vocabulary exists; the policy is new authority); (2) save-and-continue vs save-and-stop custody consequences; (3) privacy — dao.jing is public-by-address and a CESK state exposes the full live state (registers, stores, pending datoms): encryption-at-rest vs address-capability as the access model; (4) size — stores make packs large: dao.jing chunking/large-blob path and the DHT transport story. Process proposal (owner to confirm Tuesday): architect design loop (Fable via claude-cloud — her subscription resets Tuesday, or agy/argon) producing a short spec, then implementation to claude/codex/agy by capability (~4-6 days incl. tests; the file-based thin version was 2-3 days but skips dao.jing publication and custody). Also still queued from the argon gates: the module-export lift-refusal hole (lift module exports by module coordinate via host-export profiles — known-gaps row exists) as a small companion design/implementation item.\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:12062:  (R3) the abstraction taxonomy — dao.stream is an abstraction boundary, not a transport;\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:12266:Verification: orchestrator re-ran build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh from the repo root in the foreground twice: round 1 SUMMARY 37 pass 0 fail exit 0; after the fixes SUMMARY 36 assertions 36 pass 0 fail, 3 records, exit 0, git status shows no tracked-file edits. Linux x86_64 on shiva: NOT RUN (shiva read-only env check done earlier: Ubuntu 24.04.5 x86_64, gcc 13.3, cmake 3.28.3, mise 2026.