limits or preexisting transport\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1699:1. **Native continuation transport has a limited scope.** The sources support same-model register payloads and qualified transport refusal, while the module encoder currently refuses continuations. Keep this distinction visible in API docs/tests. A full portable continuation design would require separate authorization and compatibility work, not an ad hoc wasm-only decoder.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1700:2. **Absolute `pc` layout compatibility.** The register kernel's format check tests image membership, not complete receiving-layout equivalence. That suffices for grow-only same-task use; it is not proof that arbitrary imported absolute pcs mean the same thing. The transport adapter must enforce matching layout/prefix or use a separately specified rebaser (§10.2.3). Preserve legacy restore diagnostics without presenting the legacy check as stronger than it is.\\ndocs/design/dao.nao.md:148:The host may bootstrap the VM and provide transports or kernel dispatch, but\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:74:The coordinate is itself data and may be transported through a stream, then\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:112:  registry and no dispatch on a transport type: opening a coordinate is\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:141:Relation values, view values and result values are values, not transports.\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:145:them carries a reopenable transport identity — a result names nothing another\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:487:  backend's own transport.\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:515:- **The relation transport is eliminated.** The v1\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:519:  value with a transport type stamped on it. `query/relation` returns that\\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:521:  carry data — which is all the transport ever provided.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:24:about all three. The JVM transport parked inside `append!` (`ws/jvm.clj:158`\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:25:joined the send future), so D1's claim was false *for the shipped transport*;\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:34:§5.2 #6 asserted a connect-timeout cleanup the transport does not perform.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:40:narrowing question into a second transport project inside a migration plan —\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:47:D2 now state what the shipped transport does and does not do, as a limit and\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:93:   **once Phase 0 makes the JVM transport keep its own promise** (D1).\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:105:   through fake `call-fn`s and touch no transport; six (`network-*`) go through\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:138:transport limit N2 states and §8/§9 carry; this plan does not repair it.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:152:close. Both halves are values over injected functions and name no transport.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:212:| C2 | `call-fn` is invoked as `(call-fn client op args)`, `close-fn` as `(close-fn client)`; the handle names no transport | `[D]` docstring — the property that makes this plan small |\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:228:| N2 | `connect-content!` returns only after the attachment reports `/established`; a terminal lifecycle before that throws with the reason, and `:connect-timeout-ms` without either throws `{:url :timeout-ms}`. In **all three** failure cases — the terminal lifecycle, the deadline, and an interruption of the establishment loop's sleep — **before the throw**, `stream/close!` has run on the handle, which flips its phase to `:closed` so no handle, medium or state escapes to the caller and no later call could be made through it. The third was found in Phase 2's review: an interrupt propagating raw left an attached handle and its socket live while the caller held nothing, which is a failure of this clause, not of N11 (no id is allocated yet). The traffic cursor is minted **before** `attach!`, so `/established` cannot land ahead of the position that will observe it. **The limit, in this plan's own words**: on the JVM, `stream/close!` before the socket exists records a `:close-request` and does nothing else (`jvm.clj:168-173`); nothing touches the pending `buildAsync` future, and the `HttpClient` that `connect!` builds carries no connect or request timeout. So against a peer that accepts TCP and never completes the upgrade, the client-side JDK connection is **not torn down**, the peer **need not observe EOF at any time**, and the connection persists for the **process lifetime**. Each such failed open leaks one JDK connection, and `dao.jing.remote` has no handle through which to prevent it. This is the shipped transport's behaviour and this plan does not change it (§8) | `[T→D]` — `network-invalid-url-test` pins only validation (§5.2 #5); §5.2 #5 and #6 pin the throw and that nothing escapes; nothing pins teardown, because there is none |\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:229:| N3 | A call returns the remote value to the caller; a duplicate put reports `:present` over the wire; a second client sees the first's writes; content survives a server restart on a file backend; `nil`, an envelope-shaped payload, and a caller sentinel round-trip | `[T]` — transport transparency, C1/H3/H4 through a real socket; kept verbatim |\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:234:| N8 | Once the attachment is terminal (`/detached`, `/ended`, `/not-found`, `/transport-error`), every call throws with that reason; the handle is never rebound. Reattachment is the caller's: open a new coordinate | `[D]` **new** — D4 |\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:248:| S3 | A bind failure throws from `serve-content!` and retains nothing | `[D]` **new** — `serving/start!` answe